### WHERE THE 203 SIT, AND WHY — corrected attribution (2026-08-13)

WHERE. All 203 are at k=2 and they sit in exactly FOUR family words, with nothing lost and no new
words appearing:
    a    35 ->  95   (+60)        a = 3s3
    ab   40 -> 115   (+75)        b = 4s3
    ae   18 ->  70   (+52)        e = 6s3
    abe   2 ->  18   (+16)
Those four words are precisely the PURE SIDE-3 words — tilings whose every tile is a side-3 polygon.
So AL's instinct identified the right SET (he asked whether single-scale tilings had been removed);
the mechanism is different, as the tests below show. Nothing was ever removed: both catalogs keep the
same 10 pure side-1 blocks at k=1, on the same 8 family words.

WHY NOT A FILTER. The old RAW solver output has 1,114 blocks against the new 2,534, and re-pruning the
OLD RAW with the CURRENT pruner reproduces 355 exactly. The gap is therefore in the solver's own
output, upstream of every filter and of the develop/T-junction gates in build-scaled-atlas.ts.

⚑ MY FIRST ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG. I blamed the unguarded dynamic face-closure filter. That filter is
not even running here: `regular-scaled-123` has flat 180° corners, so eu_solver.cpp:1533 takes the
`has_flat` branch, disables the face and pair filters and never calls `dyn_build()`. The only
BUCKET_OK-gated machinery left in play is the CANDIDATE INDEX, and the 4-bucket union is exactly the
fix to it — under BUCKET_OK false the old single-bucket key under-approximated the candidate pool, so
the search never visited those side-3 assemblies.

GROUND TRUTH. EU_NOBUCKET=1 removes the candidate index altogether (full scan, no optimization):
    k=1 90, k=2 468, total 558, from 2,534 raw blocks — identical to the current bucketed run.
Serial this run exceeded 10 minutes; sharded at N=64 D2=16 it took 3s, so depth-2 is what made the
completeness oracle affordable. 558 is CONFIRMED COMPLETE; 355 was not.

ARE THEY REAL? Of the 203 new pruned classes, 175 carry a congruence signature (k, |det Λ|, sorted
face areas, sorted corner angles) absent from the entire old catalog, so they are new geometries and
not re-labelled duplicates; the other 28 share a signature with an old entry and are supercell/dual
duplicates of the kind the shelf's canonKey already merges. Of the 175, only 16 carry a T-JUNCTION,
which is the shelf's admission gate — the remaining 159 are single-scale side-3 tilings with no
offset, i.e. plain 3x rescalings of scale-1 tilings, and the shelf drops those by design.
So: 203 more combinatorial classes, >=175 new geometries, and ~16 that actually reach the shelf.
Example: ctrnact-02_a-2cv_2cy2_2di4_2dn2_4oo_6hm-1, k=2, six 3s3 triangles per cell, |det Λ| = 23.383,
every face area 3.897114 (= sqrt(3)/4 * 9, the side-3 triangle), T-junction true, area-certified.
